WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.642
Hi, I'm Fettle!

00:00:02.642 --> 00:00:06.372
Today: how a splat renderer that never sorts can still look steady.

00:00:09.225 --> 00:00:10.998
The new idea is in training.

00:00:10.998 --> 00:00:14.543
Push every Gaussian on a ray toward one colour, so whichever gets

00:00:14.543 --> 00:00:16.020
drawn is close to right.

00:00:19.025 --> 00:00:23.147
The usual renderer sorts by depth and blends a slice of each Gaussian.

00:00:23.147 --> 00:00:25.684
Sorting costs time, and a flipped order pops.

00:00:26.104 --> 00:00:28.228
The stochastic renderer skips the sort.

00:00:28.228 --> 00:00:32.476
Each fragment survives with its alpha, and the depth test keeps one.

00:00:32.476 --> 00:00:34.600
Right on average, noisy per frame.

00:00:36.483 --> 00:00:37.607
Here is the formula.

00:00:38.483 --> 00:00:42.104
P of i is how often Gaussian i wins the pixel, the

00:00:42.104 --> 00:00:43.311
same weight blending uses.

00:00:43.731 --> 00:00:46.492
This is how far its colour sits from the pixel's colour.

00:00:47.361 --> 00:00:49.989
SteadySplats adds this variance to the training loss.

00:00:49.989 --> 00:00:52.945
Every piece is already computed, so it is cheap.

00:00:54.758 --> 00:00:55.411
One pixel.

00:00:55.411 --> 00:00:58.673
Without the term, it blends yellow, dark green and lilac.

00:00:58.673 --> 00:01:00.631
With it, every band is olive.

00:01:01.051 --> 00:01:02.838
Both blend to the same colour.

00:01:02.838 --> 00:01:05.220
Only a stochastic renderer can tell them apart.

00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:10.119
Each pixel also borrows its neighbours' samples, weighted by how likely

00:01:10.119 --> 00:01:11.387
each sample is here.

00:01:11.807 --> 00:01:14.103
Those weights need the occluders in front.

00:01:14.103 --> 00:01:16.727
A small buffer of recent samples supplies them.

00:01:17.147 --> 00:01:21.757
Over time, the reuse collapses to a moving average per pixel, the

00:01:21.757 --> 00:01:23.294
filter temporal anti-aliasing uses.

00:01:24.508 --> 00:01:28.985
On the paper's camera paths, the variance term alone adds about seven decibels.

00:01:28.985 --> 00:01:30.362
Reuse does the rest.

00:01:32.933 --> 00:01:34.439
Sort-free splats do not pop.

00:01:34.439 --> 00:01:35.041
They boil.

00:01:35.041 --> 00:01:38.052
Most of the cure is a model whose Gaussians agree.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.784
Noise is colour variance along the ray.

00:01:42.784 --> 00:01:46.137
A loss term shrinks it, and reuse cleans up the rest.

00:01:46.557 --> 00:01:48.597
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:48.597 --> 00:01:49.179
I'm Fettle.

00:01:49.179 --> 00:01:49.471
Bye!
